(D7 — FULLY RESOLVED, re-recorded 2026-08-22 against vox 0.4.10. This
 section's half of collections-a's D7: LANGUAGE.md:2920-2924 (was
 2680-2681, then 2811-2813) now says both the variable and expression
 forms of `{...}` format interpolation "dispatch on the element's runtime
 tag, so an element renders in a hole exactly as it does printed as a
 statement" — merged into one sentence covering both. It used to appear
 in PRINT position only; as a text initializer the same slot used to
 yield a raw heap address that changed every run. vox #44 fixed the
 VARIABLE form (this probe's "text initializer" half) in every sink,
 2026-08-21. The EXPRESSION form ("{element 2 of nested}") was a distinct
 sub-case #44 did NOT reach; vox #68 fixed it too, landing in 0.4.10.
 Written as verdict lines so the probe is re-runnable; the raw values are
 the wandering part.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox D7.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   print position: [1, 2, 3]
   text initializer renders the list
   expression form renders the nested list
)
a list called flat is [1, 2, 3].
print "print position: {flat}".
a text called captured is "{flat}".
If captured is equal to "[1, 2, 3]" then, print "text initializer renders the list".
Otherwise, print "text initializer renders something else (a heap address)".
a buffer called sink is 64 bytes in size.
copy "{flat}" to sink.
a list called nested is [1, [2, 3], "four"].
a text called inner is "{element 2 of nested}".
If inner is equal to "[2, 3]" then, print "expression form renders the nested list".
Otherwise, print "expression form renders something else (a heap address)".
